{"schema":"plectis_maths_universe_excerpts_v2","paper":"erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface","excerpts":[["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:B3","One diagonal parameter suffices","<p><em> The following condition is equivalent to irrationality: </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>S</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> For a\nhypothetical rational value, let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span> be its eventual tail period and\nstarting index. Taking <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> ensures <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∣</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span>. The corresponding diagonal\ndifference is then integral, contradicting a certificate at that scale.\nConversely, irrationality and pointwise completeness provide a witness\nat every prescribed <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi></math></span>. The\nreduction uses one scale parameter; it neither establishes the\ncertificate condition nor makes one fixed scale sufficient for all\nrational values.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:B7","A sufficient condition on the LCM\ngrid","<p><em> </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>q</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>m</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi>S</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> One residue certificate\n<em>anywhere</em> on the two-multiplier LCM cone, at arbitrarily large\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi></math></span>, suffices; the diagonal choice\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> recovers Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:B3\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:B3\">6.72</a>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:B10","A finite-grid certificate\ncondition","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> and let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math></span> be finite and nonempty. Define <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></munderover><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></math></span>\nand <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. If <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup></math></span> for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></math></span> and </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo lspace=\"0.278em\" rspace=\"0.278em\">mod</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span> for some\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></math></span>. Such a test at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></math></span> for arbitrarily large <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi></math></span> implies <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>. Neither <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span> nor compatibility of every pair\nis a hypothesis. The proof, by choosing a minimum tail in the finite\nset, is given in Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:cert:b10a\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b10a\">6.56</a>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:C2sup","Unbounded Farey bounds imply\nirrationality","<p><em> The Farey-gap denominator bound at window <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>K</mi></math></span> is currently <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo>∼</mo><mn>7.96</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mn>34</mn></mrow></msup></math></span> at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>240</mn></math></span> (Prop. <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:C2-inv\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:C2-inv\">6.99</a>). If </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><munder><mo movablelimits=\"true\">sup</mo><mi>K</mi></munder><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> (that is, the\nproved exclusion bounds are arbitrarily large), then #249 follows via\nthe denominator-exclusion implication, without assuming the certificate\nsupply in Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:A10\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:A10\">6.71</a>. No unboundedness claim is\nproved or attempted in the record.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:D5cons","The lower bound and a false\nproposed upper bound","<p><em> By Prop. <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:D4-inv\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:D4-inv\">6.98</a>, the canonical dyadic\ntotient-kernel family is unconditionally <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>-dimensional at every level <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. Rationality of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi></math></span> forces an associated carry orbit with\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>-rank <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> at every level (Prop. <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:CP-02\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:CP-02\">6.90</a>). An upper bound\nindependent of <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi></math></span> for these same\ncarry ranks would contradict the lower bound. More generally, a bound\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> at some level <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> would suffice, provided it applies\nto the actual carry under the hypothetical rationality of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi></math></span>. A bound that merely grows with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi></math></span> need not contradict anything. The\ngeneric assertion for arbitrary rational coefficient series is false:\nthe <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></math></span> comparison sequence in\nSection <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#sec:mahler-defect\">10.8</a> has\nan integer carry satisfying the growth condition and the same rank lower\nbound. The incompatible integer identity and the finite-shift\ncounterexample (Observation <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:B4b-kill\">6.115</a>) remain separate\ncounterexample results. None gives a rank upper bound from rationality\nalone. The Lean proof covers the rank lower bound and counterexample\nresults, not the counterfactual upper bound.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:B12cons","Soundness of the finite carry\ntest","<p><em> For fixed <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, use the integer recurrences <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub></math></span> defined above. Test each of the\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> candidates <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. If every candidate has some\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>K</mi></math></span> for which </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≥</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>. An integral\ntail difference would give one of the initial candidates and would\nremain in the <em>open</em> strip <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> at every step.\nEquality at a boundary already excludes a candidate. This is the\nsoundness implication for a specified finite test; no bound for a\nsuccessful search depth is asserted.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:AR-04-inv","The exact totient difference on an\nLCM progression","<p><em> Put <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>.\nFor every <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, the arithmetic\nexpression in the piecewise form given below equals <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>.\nFor a divisor <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>H</mi></math></span>, this\nassertion contains a useful product formula, not merely a change of\nnotation. Set </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Here <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> because <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, so both denominators are nonzero.\nThe identity follows from <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>,\nwhich retains all primes shared by <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></span>. If every prime dividing <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi></math></span> also divides <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi></math></span>, then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, and the formula reduces to\n<span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></math></span>.\nFor example, at <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> the two gcds\nare <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>; the exact difference is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, whereas\ndiscarding the gcd factors would give <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span>. For <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>∤</mo><mi>H</mi></math></span>, that expression is the literal totient difference. Thus no\ncoprimality assumption is imposed on the full expression.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:AR-03-inv","A lower bound for the totient of a\nrough integer","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></math></span>, set\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></math></span>, and let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> be an integer all of whose prime\nfactors exceed <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi></math></span>. If <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></math></span>, then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi></math></span> has fewer than <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></math></span> distinct prime factors and </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mi>n</mi><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> If <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi></math></span> has <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi></math></span> distinct prime factors, then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></math></span>, so <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></math></span> because <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></math></span>. For the second, use <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></math></span>. These are bounds for\nrough integers in the stated size range; they are not pointwise bounds\nfor arbitrary totient values.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:AR-05-inv","Positivity of the short-window\ndifferences","<p><em> For <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></math></span> and\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThe proof treats divisor offsets and the exceptional prime-power offsets\nseparately, using multiplicativity and the rough-integer estimate of\nProposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:AR-03-inv\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:AR-03-inv\">6.79</a>.\nNo rationality hypothesis is used. Positivity of these finitely many\ncoefficients contributes to the later sign theorem; a bound for the\nremaining infinite tail is still needed to obtain that theorem.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:AR-06-inv","The accumulated sum is the\ndiscrepancy","<p><em> For all <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, the weighted diagonal sum is exactly </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThus its two-sided residue inequality is precisely <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. The equality\nfollows term by term from the definition of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></math></span>. A separate restriction on <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi></math></span>, when present, remains part of the\nhypothesis; this identity does not supply such a restricted-depth\nwitness.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:SEP-01-inv","A global-to-local identity","<p><em>\n</em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo>=</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">(</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></munderover><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mstyle></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Thus a separation\nestimate for <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></math></span> is an\nestimate for this integer translate of a multiple of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi></math></span>. The identity does not require other\nrational-approximation arguments to use this particular\ntranslate.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:SEP-02-inv","A rational approximation with an\nerror bound","<p><em> For all <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, with <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub></math></span> and the\nfinite <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></math></span> defined above,\n</em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The triangle inequality\ntransfers a separation estimate between the real tail and its rational\napproximation at the cost of this error. The bound tends to zero with\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi></math></span> for fixed <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi></math></span>; no assertion of optimality is\nmade.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:SGN-01","Unconditional positivity","<p><em> For\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></math></span>: </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> with no irrationality hypothesis; the true,\ninfinite, real translated tail difference is strictly positive\nthroughout almost the entire short window, proved unconditionally from\nProp. <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:AR-05-inv\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:AR-05-inv\">6.80</a> plus a\ndirected one-sided tail bound. Any argument about the sign of the actual\norbit (not merely its residue mod <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup></math></span>) must agree with this; specialised at\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> this gives <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:SGN-03","The residue forced by integrality","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, and assume </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> If <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>, then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable displaystyle=\"true\" columnalign=\"right left\" columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"3pt\" data-break-align=\"bottom top\" minlabelspacing=\"0em\"><mtr><mtd><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo lspace=\"0.278em\" rspace=\"0.278em\">mod</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></msup></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2.</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The later tail difference is a positive integer.\nIts negative is the representative near zero, while the least\nnonnegative residue lies near <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></msup></math></span>.\nPositivity alone therefore does not prove nonintegrality; a separate\nestimate must exclude this upper interval.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:TE-02-inv","The penultimate term of a partial\ndivisibility pattern","<p><em> Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. Suppose that </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable displaystyle=\"true\" columnspacing=\"1em\" rowspacing=\"3pt\" data-break-align=\"middle\" minlabelspacing=\"0em\"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>B</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Indeed, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></math></span>\nand <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup></math></span> force <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span>. The penultimate divisibility,\npositivity, and the bound <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup></math></span> leave\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span> as its only possible value.\nThus the modulus lies in the strict interval <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></math></span>; there is at most one\npossible power of two. The value fixed at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span> is the <em>penultimate</em>\ndifference. The terminal difference <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> instead satisfies the\nseparate upper bound in the hypotheses. These restrictions are necessary\nfor the specified pattern; the proposition does not construct such\npatterns at arbitrarily large <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:TE-03-inv","An equivalent test using two\nresidue bits","<p><em> For every <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, existence of a certificate at some depth is equivalent to\nthe following explicit condition: </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mtext>or</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing=\"1em\" rowspacing=\".2em\" columnalign=\"left left\"><mtr><mtd><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo lspace=\"0.278em\" rspace=\"0.278em\">mod</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" symmetric=\"true\"></mo></mrow></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The last interval says that the two leading bits\nare <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>01</mn></math></span> or <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>10</mn></math></span>. The proof uses the first successful\ncertificate of depth <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi></math></span> and may take\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌊</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌋</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>.\nSince <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi></math></span> depends on that unknown\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi></math></span>, the equivalence is not an a\npriori bound on the search depth. The proof uses the finite tail\nrecurrence and its size bound; it supplies no totient-specific assertion\nthat a witness exists.</em></p>"]],"source":"erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.json?v=ba7ef333d530"}
